WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:14.540
In May 2024, Google rolled out AI Overviews in its search. When you search, an answer written by AI appears at the top of the page.

2
00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:20.050
Someone asked: the cheese keeps sliding off my pizza. What should I do?

3
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:27.720
AI Overviews suggested adding a little glue to the sauce. Non-toxic glue, it said, would do.

4
00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:39.190
Reporters traced the idea back to a comment on a forum (Reddit), posted more than ten years earlier. And that comment was clearly a joke.

5
00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:46.350
So why would AI take a joke seriously? Where do its answers actually come from?

6
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:52.053
This is C2: data, models and hallucination.

7
00:00:52.403 --> 00:00:59.283
You'll learn where AI's answers come from, and why it sometimes makes things up with a straight face.

8
00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:09.147
How does an AI come to exist? Remember four words: data, training, model, answer.

9
00:01:09.497 --> 00:01:17.937
Data is huge amounts of text, pictures and sound, like all the books and exercises a student has read.

10
00:01:18.287 --> 00:01:33.247
Training means letting a program find patterns in that data, adjusting again and again, like doing exercises, checking the answers and fixing mistakes. What comes out is called a model.

11
00:01:33.597 --> 00:01:43.397
When you ask a question, the model works out an answer from the patterns it learned, like a student meeting a new exam question and answering by habit.

12
00:01:43.747 --> 00:01:52.427
Here's the key point. A model learns patterns. It is not an answer book it can look things up in.

13
00:01:53.200 --> 00:02:03.560
The pattern a chat AI learns is a special one. It's like a word chain: look at the words so far, and guess the most likely next word.

14
00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:18.190
This is a demo. Say “Twinkle, twinkle, little…” and it says “star”. It gets that right because the line appears again and again in its data, not because it has ever looked at the night sky.

15
00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:27.540
At each step there are several words that are about equally likely. So the same question, asked twice, can get two different answers.

16
00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:38.410
In one line: it learns to sound right, not to be right. Most of the time, what sounds right is right. But not always.

17
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:47.320
A mistake that sounds true is called a hallucination. Researchers at OpenAI have explained a few of the reasons.

18
00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:56.550
One: the text it trains on isn't labelled true or false. So what it learns is how to sound plausible.

19
00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:14.780
Two: some facts just don't follow any pattern, like an ordinary person's birthday. The researchers asked a chatbot for the title of one author's PhD thesis. It confidently gave three different answers, and all three were wrong.

20
00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:28.290
Three: many tests only count right answers. Leave a question blank and you get 0, but a guess might be lucky. So models learn to guess, even when they don't know.

21
00:03:29.067 --> 00:03:33.587
So AI's mistakes usually come from three places.

22
00:03:33.937 --> 00:03:40.177
One: the data was wrong to begin with, or it was a joke, like glue on pizza.

23
00:03:40.527 --> 00:03:51.807
Two: the data has very little, or nothing, on the topic, like obscure names, dates or references. So it makes up something that looks right.

24
00:03:52.157 --> 00:04:02.037
Three: the data is out of date. The world changed after training and it doesn't know, like the latest height of Mount Everest.

25
00:04:02.387 --> 00:04:13.067
Even searching the web before it answers isn't a guarantee. What it finds may be unreliable, and the writing step can still go wrong.

26
00:04:13.867 --> 00:04:19.787
Let's try a small challenge called: make the AI get it wrong. This is a demo.

27
00:04:20.137 --> 00:04:27.097
Think of an obscure question that you can check, like the year a school near you was founded. Then ask the AI.

28
00:04:27.447 --> 00:04:34.687
Now check it yourself, on the school's website, in local records, or by asking an older neighbour.

29
00:04:35.037 --> 00:04:46.117
If it gives you a specific year that doesn't match what you found, you've caught it making something up. If it honestly says it doesn't know, write that down too.

30
00:04:46.467 --> 00:04:53.547
The point isn't to prove AI is useless. It's to see which kinds of question trip it up.

31
00:04:54.334 --> 00:05:09.134
Three things to remember. AI's answers come from data and a trained model. It learns to sound right, not to be right. And anything obscure, recent or important, always check.

32
00:05:09.484 --> 00:05:20.364
A quick exercise: try to make an AI get something wrong, and write down its exact words, your evidence and your conclusion, in one line.

33
00:05:20.714 --> 00:05:29.394
So how do you check properly? Watch C4: how do you know if what AI says is right?
